(TIM-52 - spellings the compiler accepts that the manual never lists:
 `retrieve` for `get`, `stopwatch` for `timer`, `pause` for `wait`,
 `delay` for `sleep`, `timestamp` and `unixtime` for `unix`, and plural
 `hours`/`minutes`/`days`/`months`/`years` for the property names. Each is
 a reserved word a generated program can collide with, and each is a
 spelling the generator could be varying. See Discrepancy 4.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox TIM-52.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   timestamp is unix
   unixtime is unix
   hours is hour
   minutes is minute
   days, months and years are the singular properties
   the stopwatch spelling made a working timer
)
Retrieve current time into now.
a stopwatch called lap.
Start the lap.
Pause 2 milliseconds.
Delay for 3 milliseconds.
Stop the lap.
If now's timestamp is now's unix then, print "timestamp is unix".
Otherwise, print "timestamp is {now's timestamp} against unix {now's unix}".
If now's unixtime is now's unix then, print "unixtime is unix".
Otherwise, print "unixtime is {now's unixtime} against unix {now's unix}".
If now's hours is now's hour then, print "hours is hour".
Otherwise, print "hours is {now's hours} against hour {now's hour}".
If now's minutes is now's minute then, print "minutes is minute".
Otherwise, print "minutes is {now's minutes} against minute {now's minute}".
If now's days is now's day and now's months is now's month and now's years is now's year then, print "days, months and years are the singular properties".
Otherwise, print "the plural date properties disagree with the singular ones".
If lap's end time is greater than 0 then, print "the stopwatch spelling made a working timer".
Otherwise, print "the stopwatch spelling made a timer that never ran".
